  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Spring pin (Cudoniella clavus) is a small fungal species belonging to the Tricladiaceae family, characterized by its delicate fruiting bodies and ecological role in decomposition. This gelatinous fungus is commonly found in moist environments throughout New England, where it plays an important role in nutrient cycling.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous texture",
      "description": "The fruiting bodies are distinctly gelatinous and translucent, with a jelly-like consistency when moist",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Club-shaped spore-bearing structures",
      "description": "Features characteristic clavate (club-shaped) asci that give the species its scientific name 'clavus'",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "Typically produces tiny fruiting bodies, usually less than 5mm in height, often requiring magnification to observe details",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Spring pin thrives in moist, shaded environments such as decaying wood, leaf litter, and mossy areas in forests and woodlands throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its tiny size and inconspicuous appearance, Cudoniella clavus is an efficient decomposer that helps break down dead organic matter, playing a vital role in forest nutrient cycling and ecosystem health.",
